>
  <p>
    As the regulatory landscape around artificial intelligence accelerates — from the EU AI Act to NIST's 
    AI Risk Management Framework (RMF) and Singapore's Model AI Governance Framework — the demand for 
    standardized governance credentials has expanded rapidly.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <strong>Certified Artificial Intelligence Governance Professional (AIGP / CAIGP)</strong> credential 
    represents one of the foremost professional standards bridging law, technology ethics, and risk management. 
    Here is an honest breakdown of the curriculum from a field practitioner's perspective.
  </p>

  <h2>Core Pillars of the CAIGP Body of Knowledge</h2>

  <h3>1. AI Foundations & Technical Architecture</h3>
  <p>
    Understanding machine learning paradigms (supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement learning, LLMs, diffusion models), 
    data pipelines, training methodologies, and where operational risks manifest throughout the ML lifecycle.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Global Regulatory Landscape</h3>
  <p>
    A deep dive into regulatory approaches: risk-tiered frameworks (EU AI Act), voluntary consensus standards (NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 42001), 
    and sector-specific directives across financial services and healthcare.
  </p>

  <h3>3. AI Risk Management & Impact Assessments</h3>
  <p>
    Methodologies for conducting AI Impact Assessments (AIIA), establishing accountability matrices, mapping supply chain risks 
    (foundation models vs. fine-tuned downstream apps), and configuring continuous monitoring systems.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Ethical Principles into Operational Controls</h3>
  <p>
    Translating abstract concepts like "fairness", "transparency", "explainability", and "human agency" into concrete, audit-ready operational controls.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the Textbook Ends and Practice Begins</h2>
  <p>
    While the certification provides an essential legal and compliance taxonomy, real-world AI governance requires 
    socio-technical empathy. Governance is not an exercise in ticking compliance boxes; it is the organizational muscle 
    to say "no" to premature deployment, to manage stakeholder panic when a model exhibits drift, and to build 
    participatory feedback loops with affected users.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
